response:
 In a right triangle, the midpoints of the sides form a triangle whose area is one-fourth of the area of the original triangle. Given the sides of the right triangle \( \triangle ABC \) are \( AB = 15 \) units and \( AC = 24 \) units, we can find the hypotenuse \( BC \) using the Pythagorean theorem: \[
BC = \sqrt{AB^2 + AC^2} = \sqrt{15^2 + 24^2} = \sqrt{225 + 576} = \sqrt{801}
\]
The area of \( \triangle ABC \) is \[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times AB \times AC = \frac{1}{2} \times 15 \times 24 = 180 \text{ square units}
\]
Since \( \triangle DEF \) is formed by connecting the midpoints of the sides of \( \triangle ABC \), its area is one-fourth of the area of \( \triangle ABC \). So, the area of \( \triangle DEF \) is \[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{4} \times 180 = 45 \text{ square units}
\]</think> <answer> 45 </answer>

